# What must never reach a build context.
#
# Both images are built from the REPOSITORY ROOT, because .NET restore needs
# global.json, Directory.Build.props and Directory.Packages.props, and those live
# here rather than beside a project. That makes this file load-bearing: without it
# the context is the whole working tree, including a Flutter build, a node_modules
# and every bin/obj on the machine — hundreds of megabytes shipped to the daemon
# on every build, to be thrown away.

# ── Secrets. First, because this is the one that matters. ─────────────────────
# appsettings.Local.json holds the Postgres password, the JWT signing key and the
# Brevo API key. It is gitignored, so it is not in the repository — but it IS on a
# developer's disk, and a build context is not a git clone. Without this line a
# local build would bake those secrets into an image layer, where they survive
# even if a later layer deletes the file.
**/appsettings.Local.json
**/appsettings.*.Local.json
*.pfx
*.p12
*.pem
*.key
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ── Build output ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/dist/
**/out/
**/node_modules/
**/.angular/
**/.kotlin/

# A stale local build of the console. The BFF Dockerfile builds the dashboard in a
# Node stage and copies it into wwwroot; if a developer already ran `ng build` and
# the result is sitting here, it would be published alongside the fresh one and
# whichever wins is a coin toss.
Khadra.Bff/wwwroot/

# The customer app is not part of either image: the Flutter web build is static
# and served separately. Its build directory alone is tens of megabytes.
Khadra.Mobile/

# ── Version control and tooling ───────────────────────────────────────────────
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude/
.run/
.config/

# ── Things that are not inputs to a server build ──────────────────────────────
docs/
**/*.md
!README.md
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore
start-dev.ps1
**/TestResults/
